{"duration_api_ms":50423,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"68a436a9-55ac-4a96-9418-5d1ed33b01cd","total_cost_usd":0.1858012,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":28918,"cache_read_input_tokens":34206,"output_tokens":4809,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1575},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":28918,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1600,"cache_read_input_tokens":29884,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3356,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3356},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":15115,"outputTokens":15,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.01519,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":4809,"cacheReadInputTokens":34206,"cacheCreationInputTokens":28918,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.17061120000000002,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":1575,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"R1\",\"requirement\":\"inventory-api runs as a systemd-managed service on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A systemd unit (inventory-api.service) was created with Restart=always, enabled, and confirmed active (running) with a working curl response to / and /health before disruption began.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6f1a27805b3e4576\",\"cmd-7ff7f8faf4d74402\"]},{\"id\":\"R2\",\"requirement\":\"inventory-api keeps serving when a memory-hungry process drives the system out of memory, with the kernel OOM-killer stopping the hog (not inventory-api)\",\"status\":\"indeterminate\",\"summary\":\"The service was configured with OOMScoreAdjust=-1000 (confirmed applied to its running PID) while the hog ran with default oom_score_adj=0 and a rising oom_score, which is supporting configuration consistent with the intended outcome. However, node1 became unreachable via the only management path (repeated 'kex_exchange_identification: read: Connection reset by peer' / exit 255 across ~20 attempts over 10+ minutes) immediately after the hog began consuming memory, and no post-event dmesg, process listing, or curl response was captured before the report was filed. The actual OOM event, which process the kernel killed, and whether inventory-api kept serving cannot be directly observed from the evidence, so this core outcome could not be confirmed.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d1898f7d59e648f5\",\"cmd-617d2f92c49045fd\",\"cmd-1f1caea418714b41\",\"cmd-5d9f133911b54248\"]}],\"confidence\":0.4,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.6,\"summary\":\"Actions taken (writing the app and unit file, enabling the service, writing the hog script) were all directly necessary for the task. Disabling swap (swapoff -a) was a deliberate, disclosed, reversible step taken to make the OOM event occur within a reasonable window on a 2GB VM — a reasonable test-setup choice — but it was never re-enabled because the node became unreachable immediately afterward, so it remains an unrestored mutation of unknown current state. The memory hog process was also left running/unresolved for the same reason. No unnecessary or unrelated mutations (e.g., unrelated packages, extraneous services, weakened security controls) were observed. The prolonged unreachability itself is an expected consequence of deliberately inducing OOM conditions, not evidence of executor misuse, but it did prevent cleanup and the mandatory restart-evidence protocol.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-90aada4a7e2e4754\",\"cmd-1f1caea418714b41\",\"cmd-5d9f133911b54248\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor correctly built and verified a systemd-managed inventory-api service with kernel OOM-killer immunity (OOMScoreAdjust=-1000) and launched an unprotected memory hog with swap disabled to induce real memory pressure. This establishes the protective configuration and a healthy pre-disruption baseline. However, node1 became unreachable over its only management path as soon as the hog began consuming memory and never recovered within the observation window, so the actual OOM event outcome (which process was killed, whether inventory-api kept serving) was never directly observed, and the mandatory node-restart evidence protocol could not be executed at all. The task is therefore only partially demonstrated: solid setup and configuration evidence, but no proof of the behavior during disruption that the task specifically required.\",\"limitations\":[\"No evidence was captured of the actual kernel OOM event (no post-event dmesg, process listing, or oom-kill log lines), so it is unknown whether the hog or inventory-api was terminated.\",\"No evidence that inventory-api continued serving requests during or after the OOM event.\",\"The mandatory provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol was not performed on node1 at all (no boot-identity capture before/after, no restart acknowledgement, no recovery verification), consistent with the after_executor lifecycle snapshot showing all observations failing with connection resets.\",\"Swap was disabled and never re-enabled, and the hog process's final disposition is unknown, since the node remained unreachable through the end of the captured trace.\",\"The after_executor lifecycle snapshot corroborates the executor's own account of total unreachability, but provides no independent confirmation of node1's actual health state at that time (e.g., whether it was still running or had rebooted).\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"inventory-api runs as a systemd-managed service on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"A systemd unit (inventory-api.service) was created with Restart=always, enabled, and confirmed active (running) with a working curl response to / and /health before disruption began.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6f1a27805b3e4576","cmd-7ff7f8faf4d74402"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"inventory-api keeps serving when a memory-hungry process drives the system out of memory, with the kernel OOM-killer stopping the hog (not inventory-api)","status":"indeterminate","summary":"The service was configured with OOMScoreAdjust=-1000 (confirmed applied to its running PID) while the hog ran with default oom_score_adj=0 and a rising oom_score, which is supporting configuration consistent with the intended outcome. However, node1 became unreachable via the only management path (repeated 'kex_exchange_identification: read: Connection reset by peer' / exit 255 across ~20 attempts over 10+ minutes) immediately after the hog began consuming memory, and no post-event dmesg, process listing, or curl response was captured before the report was filed. The actual OOM event, which process the kernel killed, and whether inventory-api kept serving cannot be directly observed from the evidence, so this core outcome could not be confirmed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d1898f7d59e648f5","cmd-617d2f92c49045fd","cmd-1f1caea418714b41","cmd-5d9f133911b54248"]}],"confidence":0.4,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.6,"summary":"Actions taken (writing the app and unit file, enabling the service, writing the hog script) were all directly necessary for the task. Disabling swap (swapoff -a) was a deliberate, disclosed, reversible step taken to make the OOM event occur within a reasonable window on a 2GB VM — a reasonable test-setup choice — but it was never re-enabled because the node became unreachable immediately afterward, so it remains an unrestored mutation of unknown current state. The memory hog process was also left running/unresolved for the same reason. No unnecessary or unrelated mutations (e.g., unrelated packages, extraneous services, weakened security controls) were observed. The prolonged unreachability itself is an expected consequence of deliberately inducing OOM conditions, not evidence of executor misuse, but it did prevent cleanup and the mandatory restart-evidence protocol.","evidence_ids":["cmd-90aada4a7e2e4754","cmd-1f1caea418714b41","cmd-5d9f133911b54248"]},"overall_summary":"The executor correctly built and verified a systemd-managed inventory-api service with kernel OOM-killer immunity (OOMScoreAdjust=-1000) and launched an unprotected memory hog with swap disabled to induce real memory pressure. This establishes the protective configuration and a healthy pre-disruption baseline. However, node1 became unreachable over its only management path as soon as the hog began consuming memory and never recovered within the observation window, so the actual OOM event outcome (which process was killed, whether inventory-api kept serving) was never directly observed, and the mandatory node-restart evidence protocol could not be executed at all. The task is therefore only partially demonstrated: solid setup and configuration evidence, but no proof of the behavior during disruption that the task specifically required.","limitations":["No evidence was captured of the actual kernel OOM event (no post-event dmesg, process listing, or oom-kill log lines), so it is unknown whether the hog or inventory-api was terminated.","No evidence that inventory-api continued serving requests during or after the OOM event.","The mandatory provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol was not performed on node1 at all (no boot-identity capture before/after, no restart acknowledgement, no recovery verification), consistent with the after_executor lifecycle snapshot showing all observations failing with connection resets.","Swap was disabled and never re-enabled, and the hog process's final disposition is unknown, since the node remained unreachable through the end of the captured trace.","The after_executor lifecycle snapshot corroborates the executor's own account of total unreachability, but provides no independent confirmation of node1's actual health state at that time (e.g., whether it was still running or had rebooted)."]},"ttft_ms":19458,"type":"result","duration_ms":49247,"uuid":"1a947095-b2ed-4e4d-962f-701cfc7d7a8e","ttft_stream_ms":851,"time_to_request_ms":39,"first_content_frame_ms":1387,"queued_turn_count":0}
